        ],
        [
          "beklemek",
          "to wait"
        ]
      ],
      "quiz": [
        {
          "q": "How do you say «I get up early»?",
          "options": [
            "Erken kalkarım",
            "Erken kalkıyorum",
            "Erken kalktım"
          ],
          "a": 0,
          "why": "A habit takes the present simple: kalk- + -ar + -ım = kalkarım."
        },
        {
          "q": "What does «giderdik» mean?",
          "options": [
            "we used to go",
            "we will go",
            "we went"
          ],
          "a": 0,
          "why": "-erdi/-ardı expresses a past habit: giderdik = we used to go."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "u11",
      "num": 11,
      "level": "B1",
      "title": "Linking ideas",
      "goal": "Build relative clauses with the participles -en, -dik and -ecek, and report what people say.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "Turkish links ideas with <b>participles</b> placed before the noun, with no separate relative pronoun. The suffix <b>-en / -an</b> corresponds to «who» or «that» in the present: <tur>çalışan adam</tur> (the man who works)."
        },
        {
          "t": "table",
          "caption": "Three useful participles",
          "head": [
            "Form",
            "Example",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "-en / -an",
              "<tur>bekleyen kadın</tur>",
              "the woman who is waiting"
            ],
            [
              "-dik / -dık",
              "<tur>aldığım kitap</tur>",
              "the book I bought"
            ],
            [
              "-ecek / -acak",
              "<tur>gideceğimiz otel</tur>",
              "the hotel we will go to"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "t": "note",
          "kind": "info",
          "tag": "Possessive in the participle",
          "text": "With -dik and -ecek, the possessive suffix shows who performs the action: <tur>aldığım</tur> (that I bought), <tur>aldığın</tur> (that you bought), <tur>aldığı</tur> (that he / she bought), <tur>aldığımız</tur> (that we bought)."
        },
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "To report speech, you can quote directly: <tur>Ali « Yarın geleceğim » dedi.</tur> Or you can integrate the clause: <tur>Ali yarın geleceğini söyledi.</tur> (Ali said he would come tomorrow.)."
        },
        {
          "t": "ex",
          "items": [
            {
              "tr": "Kütüphanede çalışan öğrenci benim arkadaşım.",
              "fr": "The student who works in the library is my friend."
            },
            {
              "tr": "Dün aldığım kitap çok güzel.",
              "fr": "The book I bought yesterday is very good."
            },
            {
              "tr": "Gideceğimiz otel deniz kenarında.",
              "fr": "The hotel we will go to is by the sea."
            },
            {
              "tr": "Ali yarın geleceğini söyledi.",
              "fr": "Ali said he would come tomorrow."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "t": "note",
          "kind": "tip",
          "tag": "Method",
          "text": "To render a relative clause, start with the noun, then place the action as a participle in front of it, and add the possessive if needed. <tur>Le train qui arrive</tur> → <tur>gelen tren</tur>."
        }
      ],
      "vocab": [
