    {
      "id": "u13",
      "num": 13,
      "level": "B1",
      "title": "Conditions and wishes",
      "goal": "Make hypotheses with -se and express regret with keşke.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "The <b>conditional</b> is formed with <b>-se / -sa</b> followed by the person markers: <tur>gelsem</tur> (if I came), <tur>gelsen</tur> (if you came). A real condition often uses the present or the future in the main clause."
        },
        {
          "t": "table",
          "caption": "The conditional of gelmek",
          "head": [
            "Person",
            "Form",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "ben",
              "<tur>gelsem</tur>",
              "if I came"
            ],
            [
              "sen",
              "<tur>gelsen</tur>",
              "if you came"
            ],
            [
              "o",
              "<tur>gelse</tur>",
              "if he / she came"
            ],
            [
              "biz",
              "<tur>gelsek</tur>",
              "if we came"
            ],
            [
              "siz",
              "<tur>gelseniz</tur>",
              "if you came"
            ],
            [
              "onlar",
              "<tur>gelseler</tur>",
              "if they came"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "<tur>Eğer</tur> (if) is optional. For an unreal hypothesis or a regret, -se is combined with the past <b>-ydi</b>: <tur>Param olsaydı yeni bir araba alırdım.</tur> (If I had money, I would buy a new car.)."
        },
        {
          "t": "note",
          "kind": "info",
          "tag": "Unreal hypothesis",
          "text": "The full pattern is <tur>-seydi</tur> + <tur>-erdi</tur>: <tur>Daha erken çıksaydık trene yetişirdik.</tur> (If we had left earlier, we would have caught the train.). In the negative: <tur>söylemeseydim</tur> (if I had not said it): <tur>Keşke ona söylemeseydim.</tur>"
        },
        {
          "t": "note",
          "kind": "tip",
          "tag": "Keşke",
          "text": "<tur>Keşke</tur> means «if only». It often takes the past conditional: <tur>Keşke daha erken gelseydim.</tur> (If only I had come earlier.), <tur>Keşke Türkçe konuşabilseydim.</tur> (If only I could speak Turkish.)"
        },
        {
          "t": "ex",
          "items": [
            {
              "tr": "Yarın hava güzel olursa parka gideriz.",
              "fr": "If the weather is good tomorrow, we will go to the park."
            },
            {
